[Music]  it's very important for us in the west  to understand that  asia is where the action is and people  in retail will already know this  but the bulk of consumer purchase and  growth in retail is shifting towards  asia  to give you a sense for uh the scale  of china if you take every dollar that  was spent online in the united states  last year that came to roughly 500 to  600 billion  china alone last year was 1.3 trillion  dollars which means china is larger than  the us plus uk plus japan plus germany  plus spain  and all of these markets combined so we  should spend some time thinking about  what's going on in there  especially with respect to retail and to  marketing  the first thing to understand about  china is that it's a very large  population  and roughly 50 of that population  is on the cell phone and it's connected  to the internet so you are looking at  roughly 700  to 800 million users who are on the  phone  compared to about 300 million users in  the united states  and the phone is a very important  tracking device  and the phone is actually creating a  substantial  impact on the lives of of individuals  through e-commerce  the offline retail experience is quite  bad  quite poor in china offline stores have  poor quality  very little variety targetability was  very poor prices were very high  enter e-commerce alibaba jd tencent  these large companies for a user sitting  in a small town  in china who is typically  exposed to five retail stores with  potentially  say 50 different types of shirts you can  log into the phone and get access to 10  000 different shirts  available at much cheaper prices and  delivered to you within one day unlike  the united states where  we have now walled gardens in technology  where social  is within facebook and e-commerce is  within amazon and searches within google  these platforms have kind of merged in  china to produce two very large  ecosystems the alibaba ecosystem  and the tencent ecosystem and if you are  within one of these ecosystems  search social commerce logistics  payments etc are seamlessly blended  together  two interesting things about the social  media and retail intersection  in china are worth noting one is  that within wechat which is a large  social media platform  um there is now the ability of every  small retailer  to have a storefront that is seamlessly  connected  to the social enterprise or the social  network  and that storefront will allow you to  generate engagement with your users  and convert that engagement into a  selling opportunity  another aspect that is worth noting is  what is coming out of china referred to  as social commerce  and the most famous company that is  doing this is a company called pin duo  dual  there is a huge desire by companies in  order to get engagement  advertising and and messaging amongst  users  and the insight of this company is why  don't we  just pay users to generate  engagement with their friends and this  always existed in marketing this we call  it referral marketing  but the way they implemented it was an  interesting business model  you would get a reduction in the price  of any product that you want  as a function of the number of users  that you bring in or alternatively you  could get a very massive price reduction  if you posted  a promotion sent by that brand to your  social media feed  etc so they have been able to bring down  the prices of a large number of products  quite dramatically and making it  affordable to users in tier two cities  in china which is where the massive  opportunity  arises  if you look at the united states if you  take  all the dollars that spend on retail and  ask what proportion of that is in  e-commerce it's roughly 12  china it's about 16-17 percent one way  to think about it just eighty-three  percent of the market is still open  and it's a massive market so there's  lots of room for  lots of companies including the chinese  companies  it's not easy however because it's a  local market  and models that may work in the west  will not work in china without some  porting and some brands have done a  great job of this  such as apple or walmart but some brands  have failed too for example amazon  failed in in in china so uh  with the right kind of localization  strategy it is possible  to win in a very large market  now the risk is twofold china is a  country where government  regulation is a substantial source of  investor risk  and as regulation changes or the  government changes its stance  on protectionist laws or how they think  about  e-commerce or the propagation of content  that can have  a massive impact on the business so  one has to go in with their eyes wide  open a  second source of risk is uh competition  with incumbents  is not easy for example in e-commerce  uh you have two ecosystems which is  vertically integrated into content  into payments into logistics uh into  social media  and into an advertising business and  these  competitors are fearsome they understand  the local market well  and it's not that easy to uh to compete  the marketplace is large enough  to allow for many players and i think  the general view  of a lot of businesses that are working  in this area  is of collaboration rather than  competition  i think we should all think of it as a  massive market

